      "title": "Explicit Beurling-transform counterexamples in an analytic annihilator",
      "description": "<p>Both endpoint questions in the bilinear analytic annihilator of Hayman and Lingham, Problem 7.72, have negative answers under the printed negative-kernel convention. The norm-one input v(z)=z/bar(z) on the unit disc annihilates every integrable analytic test function, while its Beurling transform is 1+log|z|^2 and is essentially unbounded. Explicit polynomials v_m(z)=z^2 sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(1-|z|^2)^j lie in the same annihilator, have norm one and have transform norm H_m, the m-th harmonic number. Each fixed polynomial has bounded transform, but no common uniform constant exists.</p><p>This is an exposition and independent explicit verification of consequences of classical formulas, with a self-contained proof. Iwaniec published the exact nonpolynomial witness, output and primitive in 1986. Gill published the general positive-angular transform formula in 2010; the polynomial formula and harmonic-number norm follow by elementary specialization. The 2019 Hayman-Lingham book attributes resolution of Problem 7.72 to Volberg. Its cited reference 52 is the 2008 Banuelos-Janakiraman finite-p paper; the current audit did not locate an explicit full-target account there or a separate Volberg proof. This limitation does not refute the book attribution. No new solution, witness, transform mechanism, first polynomial construction, first harmonic-number calculation or current worldwide openness is claimed. Priority for an earlier explicit combined statement in the exact annihilator notation remains unestablished.</p><p>The supporting archive preserves the six original submitted files and explicit authored mathematical and priority reviews, source-reading receipts, manuscript source and finite verification programs. The main argument treats all analytic area-L1 tests, principal values almost everywhere, weak derivatives at the origin and boundary, and essential rather than pointwise supremum norms. Root exact coefficient checks, an independent polynomial adversary and a distinct weak-PDE adversary are replayed in normal and both optimized Python modes. A fresh reconstruction begun without candidate history also recovered the witnesses; its optional numerical quadratures are empirical checks, not certified error bounds or automatically replayed exact results. An earlier purported blind family inherited candidate history; it is retained with an explicit exposure correction and is not counted as blind. The original historical priority/access statements are superseded by current source-based adjudication. Primary source bodies, provider responses, caches and raw inspection material are omitted from the portable archive. AI tools were used extensively in research, solving, verification, literature review, drafting and internal adversarial review. This work is unrefereed; no external human peer review, journal acceptance or formal proof-assistant certification is claimed.</p>",
